2026-06-30 15:49:10,417 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-88715adc-cc07-4a84-b73c-5df9380c1f41', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: disease\nsdrf_column: characteristics[disease]\nontology: EFO:0000408, MONDO, DOID, PATO\ndescription: Disease state of the sample.\ninstructions: For healthy/normal samples use "normal" (PATO:0000461). For diseased\n  samples use MONDO or DOID terms. Return null only if completely unstated.\n  Most XL-MS samples are "normal" unless explicitly studying a disease model.\n\nterm: phenotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phenotype]\nontology: EFO:0000651, PATO, EFO\ndescription: Observable characteristics or traits (drug sensitivity, molecular markers,\n  expression phenotypes, environmental responses).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. This captures how the sample behaves/appears\n  (distinct from disease diagnosis).\n\nterm: treatment\nsdrf_column: characteristics[treatment]\nontology: EFO:0000727, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Treatment or perturbation applied to the sample (drug, stimulus,\n  environmental stress, growth condition change).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable. Examples: "doxycycline\n  treatment", "heat shock", "DMSO vehicle control".\n\nterm: compound\nsdrf_column: characteristics[compound]\nontology: CHEBI:24431, CHEBI, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Chemical compound or drug applied to the sample.\ninstructions: Use ChEBI if possible. Return null if no compound treatment. Do NOT\n  include the crosslinking reagent here — that goes in comment[cross-linker].\n\nterm: dose\nsdrf_column: characteristics[dose]\nontology: NCIT:C25488\ndescription: Dose or concentration of compound treatment.\npattern: number + unit (e.g. 10 mg/kg, 50 uM, 100 nM, 10 mg/mL)\ninstructions: Return null if no compound treatment or dose not stated.\n\nterm: genetic modification\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genetic modification]\nontology: EFO:0000510, EFO\ndescription: Method of genetic modification (knockout, knockdown, overexpression,\n  transduction). Describes HOW the modification was done.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: perturbation\nsdrf_column: characteristics[perturbation]\nontology: PRIDE:0000700\ndescription: Type of perturbation applied (environmental/experimental perturbation).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: depletion\nsdrf_column: characteristics[depletion]\nontology: PRIDE:0000020\ndescription: Whether abundant protein depletion was performed.\nallowed_values: no depletion, depletion, not applicable\ninstructions: Return "no depletion" if not mentioned. Return "depletion" if\n  high-abundance protein removal is described (e.g. albumin depletion).\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nInteraction of Human Cytomegalovirus pUL52 with major components of the viral DNA encapsidation network underlines its essential role in genome cleavage-packaging\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nCleavage of human cytomegalovirus (HCMV) genomes and their packaging into capsids requires at least seven essential viral proteins, yet it is not completely understood how these proteins cooperate to accomplish this task. Besides the portal protein pUL104 and the terminase subunits pUL51, pUL56, and pUL89, the UL52 protein is also necessary for HCMV genome encapsidation, however, knowledge about pUL52 is scant. In the absence of pUL52, viral concatemers are not cleaved into unit-length genomes and no DNA-filled capsids are observed, yet no viral or cellular proteins interacting with pUL52 have been identified that would explain how pUL52 exerts its essential role in the HCMV infection cycle. In this study, we aimed at a comprehensive definition of pUL52-interacting proteins in infected cells. Using suitable HCMV mutants, we employed three complementary state-of-the-art proteomic approaches, namely biotin ligase-dependent proximity labeling, affinity purification and cross-linking mass spectrometry. These experiments, combined with thorough validation by immunoblotting, pointed to several viral DNA-associated proteins and key players pivotal for genome encapsidation as interactors of pUL52. The most noticeable direct pUL52 interaction partners were the terminase subunits pUL56 and pUL89 as well as the portal protein pUL104. Hence, we suggest a model of pUL52 function in which pUL52 mediates association of HCMV genomes with the terminase subunits and the capsid portal. Taken together, our data contribute to the understanding of an essential viral process previously recognized as a prominent antiviral target. Disturbing the identified pUL52 interactions may provide a starting point to develop novel antiviral medication.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor HA and Streptactin pull-down 2.5 × 106 HFF were seeded per T75 flask. The next day  the cell culture medium was replaced by serum-free medium. Cells were then infected at an MOI of 0.4 with virus mutants HS-UL52-B2 or HCMV-GFP as control (for HA pull-down), or HD-UL52-SF or the HD control virus (for Streptactin pull-down), followed by centrifugal enhancement. 4-6 h later, the inocula were replaced by complete medium and cells were cultivated until day 4 post infection. Cells were harvested and resuspended in 600 µl ice-cold lysis buffer  The cell lysates were incubated on ice for 15 min, and additionally at -80 °C for 1 h. Lysates were used for pull-down experiments by adding either 60 µl of equilibrated protein A-sepharose bead slurry and 2.5 μg of HA antibody  per sample for HA pull-down, or 60 µl of equilibrated Streptactin sepharose beads for Streptactin pull-down. Samples were subsequently incubated at 4 °C overnight with constant mixing. Beads were then washed twice with lysis buffer, once with lysis buffer lacking detergents, and once with PBS. Proteins enriched by HA pull-down were subjected to MS analysis. BioID assay. HFF were seeded in T75 flasks and infected the next day utilizing an MOI of 0.4 plus centrifugal enhancement as described above. For BioID assay with subsequent MS analysis, virus mutant HS-UL52-B2 was employed with HS-B2 as control virus. Biotin ) was added 24 h prior to lysis. Cells were harvested 4 dpi in BioID lysis buffer. Benzonase treatment was done with 125 U/ml at 4°C for 30 min with constant mixing, and insoluble material was removed by centrifuging. Samples to be analyzed by MS were deployed directly for Streptavidin pull-down. To enrich biotinylated proteins, 60 µl of equilibrated Streptavidin sepharose was added to the samples and incubation was done overnight at 4 °C on a tumbling wheel. Beads were washed once with wash buffer WB1 at RT, once with WB2, once with WB3 and twice with WB4 and three times with WB5 before being further processed for MS. For both HA-pull-down and Bio-ID samples, experiments were designed in biological triplicates, with suitable specificity controls. The material was denatured in 8 M urea in 50 mM TEAB buffer, reduced for 30 min with 4 mM DTT and alkylated with 40 mM CAA at ambient temperature for 30 min in the dark. Lys-C was added at concentration of 1:100 and incubated for 2 h at RT. After that, the samples were diluted 4-fold with TEAB, and Trypsin was added at 1:25 and incubated overnight to digest proteins. Following this, the samples were desalted using stage-tips purification employing C18 empore disks .  Bottom-up proteomic samples were measured on an Orbitrap Fusion Tribrid instrument with a Dionex UltiMate 3000 system connected to a PepMap C-18 trap-column and an in-house-packed C18 column  at 300 nL/min flow rate and 180 min gradient lengths. The MS1 scans were performed in the orbitrap using 120,000 resolution. The MS2 scans were acquired in the ion trap with standard AGC target settings, an intensity threshold of 1e4 and maximum injection time of 40 ms. A 1 s cycle time was set between master scans. Cross-linking of protein complexes followed by MS analysis. HFF were seeded in 25 15-cm dishes and cultivated until 90% confluence. 16 h prior to infection the medium was replaced by serum-free medium, and infection was done with HCMV mutant HD-UL52-SF, using volumes of virus-containing supernatant from HD-UL52-SF-infected cells  that ensured infection of all cells in the culture. The inoculum was replaced by complete medium 5 hpi and HFF were cultivated for another 4 days. Harvesting was performed as described above. Pre-clear was performed using 200 µl of agarose beads for 3 h at 4 °C with constant mixing on a tumbling wheel, and pull-down was done for 3 h at 4° C by adding 200 µl of equilibrated Streptactin beads to the cleared lysate. The Streptactin matrix including bound protein complexes was washed as outlined above and resuspended in 500 µl cross-linking buffer . DSSO was dissolved in DMSO and added to a final concentration of 0.5 mM, and cross-linking was carried out for 20 min at RT. This was repeated once, resulting in a final DSSO concentration of 1 mM. The cross-linking reaction was quenched with Tris-HCl pH 8.0 for 30 min at RT, and the supernatant was removed by centrifugation of beads at 500 × g for 3 min.  Cross-linked peptides were measured on an Orbitrap Fusion Lumos Tribrid system  equipped with a FAIMS Pro Duo interface operating with Xcalibur 4.6 and Tune 4.0. For this, sample was loaded with an online-connected Ultimate 3000 RSLC nano LC system  onto a 50 cm analytical, in-house packed reverse-phase column and separated with a 180 min gradient going from 0.1% w/v FA in water (buffer A) to 0.1% w/v FA in 80% v/v ACN (buffer B) at a flow rate of 250 nl/min. FAIMS compensation voltages were alternated between -50, -60 and -75 V. Cross-linked peptides were detected using a stepped-HCD-MS2 method.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
