eded in T75 flasks and infected the next day utilizing an MOI of 0.4 plus centrifugal enhancement as described above. For BioID assay with subsequent MS analysis, virus mutant HS-UL52-B2 was employed with HS-B2 as control virus. Biotin ) was added 24 h prior to lysis. Cells were harvested 4 dpi in BioID lysis buffer. Benzonase treatment was done with 125 U/ml at 4°C for 30 min with constant mixing, and insoluble material was removed by centrifuging. Samples to be analyzed by MS were deployed directly for Streptavidin pull-down. To enrich biotinylated proteins, 60 µl of equilibrated Streptavidin sepharose was added to the samples and incubation was done overnight at 4 °C on a tumbling wheel. Beads were washed once with wash buffer WB1 at RT, once with WB2, once with WB3 and twice with WB4 and three times with WB5 before being further processed for MS. For both HA-pull-down and Bio-ID samples, experiments were designed in biological triplicates, with suitable specificity controls. The material was denatured in 8 M urea in 50 mM TEAB buffer, reduced for 30 min with 4 mM DTT and alkylated with 40 mM CAA at ambient temperature for 30 min in the dark. Lys-C was added at concentration of 1:100 and incubated for 2 h at RT. After that, the samples were diluted 4-fold with TEAB, and Trypsin was added at 1:25 and incubated overnight to digest proteins. Following this, the samples were desalted using stage-tips purification employing C18 empore disks .  Bottom-up proteomic samples were measured on an Orbitrap Fusion Tribrid instrument with a Dionex UltiMate 3000 system connected to a PepMap C-18 trap-column and an in-house-packed C18 column  at 300 nL/min flow rate and 180 min gradient lengths. The MS1 scans were performed in the orbitrap using 120,000 resolution. The MS2 scans were acquired in the ion trap with standard AGC target settings, an intensity threshold of 1e4 and maximum injection time of 40 ms. A 1 s cycle time was set between master scans. Cross-linking of protein